### 1.  How the scalar (or pseudoscalar) Wilson coefficient enters the branching ratio  

For the leptonic decays  

\[
B_q\;(q=s,d)\;\longrightarrow\;\mu^{+}\mu^{-}
\]

the (CP‑averaged) branching fraction can be written in a very compact form (neglecting the tiny SM scalar‑ and pseudoscalar pieces)

\[
\boxed{
\mathcal B(B_q\!\to\!\mu^{+}\mu^{-})
=
\mathcal B(B_q\!\to\!\mu^{+}\mu^{-})_{\!{\rm SM}}^{\;(C_{10})}
\;\Bigl[\,1
+\underbrace{\frac{m_{B_q}^{2}}{4m_{\mu}^{2}}}_{\displaystyle R_q}
\frac{|C_{S}|^{2}+|C_{P}|^{2}}{|C_{10}^{\rm SM}|^{2}}
\Bigr] .
}
\tag{1}
\]

* \(C_{10}^{\rm SM}\simeq -4.2\) is the (real) SM Wilson coefficient of the axial–vector operator.  
* \(R_q\equiv m_{B_q}^{2}/(4m_{\mu}^{2})\) is a **helicity‑enhancement factor**: it is ≃ 6 × 10² for the \(B_s\) and ≃ 6.3 × 10² for the \(B_d\).  
* We have dropped the SM scalar/pseudoscalar pieces because they are \(\mathcal O(m_\mu m_b/m_W^{2})\) and numerically negligible.

Equation (1) shows that a **real scalar coefficient** (or equally a real pseudoscalar coefficient) contributes quadratically; the interference term with the SM amplitude is absent because the SM scalar matrix element vanishes.

---

### 2.  Imposing a ≤ 10 % deviation

We demand that the NP piece change the SM prediction by **no more than 10 %**, i.e.

\[
\frac{\Delta\mathcal B}{\mathcal B_{\rm SM}} \equiv
\frac{R_q}{|C_{10}^{\rm SM}|^{2}}\,
|C_{S(P)}|^{2}\;\le\;0.10 .
\]

Solving for the magnitude of the coefficient gives

\[
\boxed{
|C_{S(P)}|\;\le\;
\sqrt{\,0.10\; \frac{|C_{10}^{\rm SM}|^{2}}{R_q}\,}\, .
}
\tag{2}
\]

The only numbers that differ between the two mesons are the helicity factors \(R_q\).

---

### 3.  Numerical values of the helicity factor  

\[
\begin{aligned}
m_{\mu} &= 105.658\;\text{MeV}=0.105658\;\text{GeV},\\[2pt]
m_{B_s} &= 5.3668\;\text{GeV}, &
R_{s} &=
\frac{m_{B_s}^{2}}{4m_{\mu}^{2}}
= \frac{(5.3668)^{2}}{4(0.105658)^{2}}
\;\simeq\; 6.45\times10^{2},\\[6pt]
m_{B_d} &= 5.2796\;\text{GeV}, &
R_{d} &=
\frac{m_{B_d}^{2}}{4m_{\mu}^{2}}
= \frac{(5.2796)^{2}}{4(0.105658)^{2}}
\;\simeq\; 6.25\times10^{2}.
\end{aligned}
\]

---

### 4.  Bounds on a **single real** scalar (or pseudoscalar) coefficient  

Insert \(|C_{10}^{\rm SM}| = 4.2\) and the two values of \(R_q\) into Eq. (2):

#### 4.1  \(B_s\to\mu^{+}\mu^{-}\)

\[
\begin{aligned}
|C_{S(P)}|_{\!B_s}
&\le
\sqrt{0.10\;\frac{(4.2)^{2}}{6.45\times10^{2}}}
= \sqrt{0.10\;\frac{17.64}{645}} \\
&= \sqrt{\frac{1.764}{6450}}
\;\approx\; 1.65\times10^{-2}.
\end{aligned}
\]

#### 4.2  \(B_d\to\mu^{+}\mu^{-}\)

\[
\begin{aligned}
|C_{S(P)}|_{\!B_d}
&\le
\sqrt{0.10\;\frac{(4.2)^{2}}{6.25\times10^{2}}}
= \sqrt{0.10\;\frac{17.64}{625}} \\
&= \sqrt{\frac{1.764}{6250}}
\;\approx\; 1.68\times10^{-2}.
\end{aligned}
\]

---

### 5.  Final quoted limits  

\[
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
|C_S| \;( \text{or } |C_P|) &\;<\; 1.7\times10^{-2}\qquad\text{(from } B_s\to\mu^+\mu^-),\\[4pt]
|C_S| \;( \text{or } |C_P|) &\;<\; 1.7\times10^{-2}\qquad\text{(from } B_d\to\mu^+\mu^-).
\end{aligned}
}
\]

Because the two bounds differ only at the **few‑percent level**, the more precise measurement of the \(B_s\) channel typically dominates the combined constraint.  

*If one assumes the same (real) coefficient contributes to both decays, the **strongest** of the two numbers—\(1.65\times10^{-2}\)—should be used as the universal bound.*

---

### 6.  Quick sanity check  

Take the bound \(|C_S|=1.7\times10^{-2}\).  
The NP contribution to the branching ratio is

\[
\frac{\Delta\mathcal B}{\mathcal B_{\rm SM}}
=
\frac{R_s}{|C_{10}^{\rm SM}|^{2}}\,|C_S|^{2}
\approx
\frac{645}{17.64}\,(1.7\times10^{-2})^{2}
\simeq 0.10,
\]

i.e. exactly the 10 % limit we imposed. The same exercise for the \(B_d\) case gives 0.099 ≈ 10 %.

Thus a **single real scalar (or pseudoscalar) Wilson coefficient larger than ≈ 0.017 would push the branching fraction of either \(B_s\) or \(B_d\) beyond the 10 % tolerance** relative to the SM predictions \(\mathcal B(B_s)=3.66\times10^{-9}\) and \(\mathcal B(B_d)=1.06\times10^{-10}\).

### 1.  Where the different couplings appear in the low‑energy effective Hamiltonian  

For the FCNC transition  

\[
b\to q\ell^{+}\ell^{-}\qquad(q=s,d)
\]

the SM contribution is written after integrating out the heavy fields \((W^{\pm},\,Z,\,t)\) as  

\[
\boxed{
\mathcal H_{\rm eff}\;=\;
-\frac{4G_{F}}{\sqrt2}\;
\frac{\alpha_{\rm em}}{4\pi}\;
V_{tb}V_{tq}^{*}
\sum_{i} C_{i}(\mu)\, Q_{i}(\mu)
+\text{h.c.}}
\tag{1}
\]

* **\(G_{F}\)** already contains **two powers of the SU(2) gauge coupling**:  

  \[
  G_{F}=\frac{\sqrt2\,g^{2}}{8M_{W}^{2}}
  \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
  \frac{4G_{F}}{\sqrt2}=
  \frac{g^{2}}{2M_{W}^{2}} .
  \]

* **\(\alpha_{\rm em}/4\pi\)** in front of the operator basis is conventionally **factored out** because the semileptonic FCNC operators are generated by a **photon or \(Z\) propagator** that couples to the lepton pair. In other words, after the loop has been performed one obtains a factor \(\alpha_{\rm em}\) from the neutral‑boson–lepton vertex; pulling it out of the Wilson coefficients makes the remaining \(C_{i}\) dimensionless numbers of order unity (or, more precisely, of order the Inami–Lim loop functions).

Hence the full amplitude contains **four** coupling factors:

1. **Two powers of the weak SU(2) coupling** hidden in \(G_{F}\) (i.e. \(g^{2}\)).
2. **One factor of the electromagnetic coupling** \(\alpha_{\rm em}\) that couples the neutral boson to the lepton pair.
3. **One extra loop factor** \(1/(16\pi^{2})\) that comes from the integration over the internal momentum of the penguin or box diagram.

Putting them together we have, schematically,

\[
\mathcal A(b\to q\ell^{+}\ell^{-})
\;\sim\;
\underbrace{\frac{g^{2}}{M_{W}^{2}}}_{\text{from }G_{F}}
\;
\underbrace{\frac{\alpha_{\rm em}}{4\pi}}_{\text{lepton vertex}}
\;
\underbrace{\frac{1}{16\pi^{2}}}_{\text{one‑loop}}
\;
V_{tb}V_{tq}^{*}
\;
\underbrace{F_{\rm IL}(x_{t})}_{\text{Inami–Lim function}} .
\tag{2}
\]

If we rewrite the weak coupling in terms of the **weak fine‑structure constant**  

\[
\alpha_{w}\equiv\frac{g^{2}}{4\pi},
\]

and combine the two explicit loop denominators, Eq. (2) becomes

\[
\boxed{
\mathcal A\;\sim\;
\frac{\alpha_{w}}{4\pi}\;
\frac{\alpha_{\rm em}}{4\pi}\;
V_{tb}V_{tq}^{*}\;
F_{\rm IL}(x_{t}) } .
\tag{3}
\]

Equation (3) exhibits **one power of \(\alpha_{w}\)** (coming from the two \(W\)–fermion vertices) **and one power of \(\alpha_{\rm em}\)** (from the neutral‑boson–lepton vertex), each multiplied by a **loop factor** \(1/(4\pi)\).  

When we square the amplitude to obtain the decay **rate**, we get

\[
|\mathcal A|^{2}\;\sim\;
\underbrace{\Bigl(\frac{\alpha_{w}}{4\pi}\Bigr)^{2}}_{\text{weak‑loop suppression}}
\;
\underbrace{\Bigl(\frac{\alpha_{\rm em}}{4\pi}\Bigr)^{2}}_{\text{electromagnetic‑loop suppression}}
\;
|V_{tb}V_{tq}^{*}|^{2}\;
|F_{\rm IL}(x_{t})|^{2}.
\tag{4}
\]

Equation (4) is the **most transparent way** to display the hierarchy of suppressions:

* **\((\alpha_{w}/4\pi)^{2}\)** – the **gauge‑loop** part, i.e. two weak vertices plus the momentum‑space loop integration.
* **\((\alpha_{\rm em}/4\pi)^{2}\)** – the **photon/Z‑to‑lepton** part; it is not a “loop’’ factor in the sense of a Feynman integral, but it is conventionally factored out of the Wilson coefficients and therefore appears as a separate squared coupling.
* **\(|F_{\rm IL}(x_{t})|^{2}\)** – the Inami–Lim function encodes the dependence on the heavy masses (top‑quark, \(W\), \(Z\)). For \(x_{t}=m_{t}^{2}/M_{W}^{2}\simeq4.6\) one finds \(|F_{\rm IL}| \sim \mathcal O(4)\), i.e. an **order‑one to few‑times‑unity enhancement** that partially compensates the small couplings.

If one wishes to suppress the notation even further and write everything in terms of a **single weak factor**, one may absorb the explicit \(\alpha_{\rm em}\) into the definition of the Wilson coefficient and quote the **overall scaling** of the *branching ratio* as  

\[
\boxed{
\mathcal B(b\to q\ell^{+}\ell^{-})\;\propto\;
\Bigl(\frac{\alpha_{w}}{4\pi}\Bigr)^{2}
\;\times\;
\underbrace{\Bigl(\frac{\alpha_{\rm em}}{4\pi}\Bigr)^{2}}_{\text{often omitted in “loop‑count’’ discussions}}
\;
|V_{tb}V_{tq}^{*}|^{2}
\;|F_{\rm IL}|^{2}.
}
\tag{5}
\]

In many phenomenological papers the **electromagnetic factor** \(\alpha_{\rm em}/4\pi\) is taken to be *universal* and therefore factored out of the discussion of “loop suppression’’; the authors then quote the suppression as **\(\alpha_{w}^{2}/(4\pi)^{2}\)**. This convention is perfectly acceptable as long as one remembers that the **full rate still carries an additional \((\alpha_{\rm em}/4\pi)^{2}\)** factor.

---

### 2.  Comparison of the two ways of writing the suppression  

| Description | Formula | What is shown explicitly |
|-------------|----------|--------------------------|
| **“Pure weak‑loop’’ language** | \((g^{2}/16\pi^{2})^{2}\times|F_{\rm IL}|^{2}\) | Two weak vertices **and** the loop integration are kept together; the electromagnetic coupling is hidden inside the definition of the operator (absorbed into the Wilson coefficient). |
| **“Full‑factor’’ language** | \((\alpha_{w}/4\pi)^{2}(\alpha_{\rm em}/4\pi)^{2}\,|F_{\rm IL}|^{2}\) | Separates the **electromagnetic** coupling that couples to the lepton pair from the **weak loop** part. Makes it clear that the *rate* is doubly suppressed: once by the weak loop and once by the electromagnetic coupling. |
| **“Compact’’ language (common in flavour‑physics reviews)** | \(\alpha_{w}^{2}/(4\pi)^{2}\times \text{(CKM)}^{2}\times|F_{\rm IL}|^{2}\) | Electromagnetic factor is omitted because it is universal for all semileptonic FCNC operators; the quoted suppression refers only to the *weak* part of the amplitude. |

All three are mathematically equivalent; the choice is a matter of **convention** and of what one wishes to emphasise:

* If the goal is to **compare the size of different SM FCNC amplitudes** (e.g. \(b\to s\ell^{+}\ell^{-}\) versus \(b\to s\nu\bar\nu\)), pulling out the universal \(\alpha_{\rm em}\) factor is convenient, because the neutrino mode does **not** carry that factor.
* If one wants to **illustrate the loop counting** in a pedagogical way, writing \((g^{2}/16\pi^{2})^{2}\) (or \((\alpha_{w}/4\pi)^{2}\)) makes the origin of the suppression crystal‑clear.
* When presenting **numerical estimates** it is safest to keep the full expression (4) or (5) and insert the actual values of \(\alpha_{w},\,\alpha_{\rm em}\), the CKM elements, and the Inami–Lim function.

---

### 3.  Bottom‑line answer to the question  

1. **Yes**, the SM amplitude for \(b\to s(d)\,\ell^{+}\ell^{-}\) contains **two powers of the weak SU(2) coupling** (the two charged–current \(W\)-vertices) **and one loop factor** \(1/(16\pi^{2})\). In terms of \(\alpha_{w}=g^{2}/4\pi\) this reads \(\displaystyle \mathcal A\sim (\alpha_{w}/4\pi)\times(\text{CKM})\times F_{\rm IL}\).

2. **The electromagnetic factor \(\alpha_{\rm em}/4\pi\) appears separately** because the neutral boson that finally couples to the lepton pair supplies an extra coupling to the leptons. In the standard effective‑Hamiltonian notation it is factored out in front of the Wilson coefficients. Therefore the **rate** carries **both** \((\alpha_{w}/4\pi)^{2}\) **and** \((\alpha_{\rm em}/4\pi)^{2}\) factors.

3. **Writing the branching‑ratio suppression as \(\alpha_{w}^{2}/(4\pi)^{2}\)** (i.e. \((g^{2}/16\pi^{2})^{2}\)) is a perfectly transparent and commonly used convention **provided one remembers that the overall normalization also contains the universal \(\alpha_{\rm em}^{2}\) factor** that has been pulled out of the Wilson coefficients.  

   If you want to be completely explicit, the most transparent form is  

   \[
   \boxed{ \mathcal B(b\to q\ell^{+}\ell^{-})\;\propto\;
          \Bigl(\frac{g^{2}}{16\pi^{2}}\Bigr)^{2}
          \Bigl(\frac{\alpha_{\rm em}}{4\pi}\Bigr)^{2}
          |V_{tb}V_{tq}^{*}|^{2}
          |F_{\rm IL}(x_{t})|^{2} } .
   \]

   This makes it evident that the tiny SM branching fractions (≈ \(10^{-9}\) for \(B_{s}\to\mu^{+}\mu^{-}\)) arise from **two weak couplings**, **one electromagnetic coupling**, **two loop suppressions**, and the **CKM factor**, all of which are partially compensated by the large top‑mass enhancement encoded in \(F_{\rm IL}\).

In summary, the loop‑counting statement “\(\sim \alpha_{w}^{2}/(4\pi)^{2}\)’’ is **correct** as a shorthand for the weak‑loop suppression, but the **full rate** must include the extra \((\alpha_{\rm em}/4\pi)^{2}\) factor and the Inami–Lim mass‑dependent enhancement. Both conventions are equivalent; the choice depends on whether you want to keep the electromagnetic coupling explicit or absorb it into the definition of the Wilson coefficients.
